Achmage Workbench — rhwp v0.9.0 form of a 0.8.7 engine patch (third_party/hop/patches-0.9.0).
Origin: Ieum 0018 (vendor/ieumoffice/vendor/rhwp-core/patches/0018-memo-support.patch; original author pblsketch <239486537+pblsketch@users.noreply.github.com>) — the two hunks upstream did not take
Status: ported
Differs from the 0.8.7 form: 0.9.0 has Ieum 0018's memo support upstream except its password policy: a password HWPX opened with its password reads as not encrypted, and memos of any password document are editable (upstream: "a password only guards opening"). This patch restores the 0.8.7 behaviour Workbench promises: `header.encrypted` (and flag 0x02) after decrypting a password HWPX, and `document_protected()` = distribution OR encrypted, so memos of a password document are read-only (the AI agent's memo guard reads `encrypted`; the app never writes a password document back with its password). The HWP5 writer still clears the flag when it writes a plain document (serializer/cfb_writer.rs). Upstream's test memos_in_an_opened_password_document_are_editable is replaced by memos_in_an_opened_password_document_are_read_only, and tests/hwpx_password_fixture.rs now asserts the decrypted HWPX IS marked encrypted (upstream: not).
Regression test: tests/cases/issue_8017_memo_api.rs memos_in_an_opened_password_document_are_read_only; app: apps/host/src/hwp-save-090.test.ts (c)

From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: h4b <h4b@localhost>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:40:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Workbench: a password document stays protected (Ieum 0018 password hunks on 0.9.0)

---
 src/document_core/queries/memo_query.rs |  7 ++++---
 src/parser/hwpx/mod.rs                  | 11 ++++++++++-
 tests/cases/issue_8017_memo_api.rs      | 22 +++++++++-------------
 tests/hwpx_password_fixture.rs          |  4 +++-
 4 files changed, 26 insertions(+), 18 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/document_core/queries/memo_query.rs b/src/document_core/queries/memo_query.rs
index 09bef23f1..f307ae5af 100644
--- a/src/document_core/queries/memo_query.rs
+++ b/src/document_core/queries/memo_query.rs
@@ -998,10 +998,11 @@ fn hits_in_any(
 }
 
 impl DocumentCore {
-    /// A distribution document is read-only in Hancom. A password only guards
-    /// opening: once opened, the document edits like any other, memos included.
+    /// A distribution document is read-only in Hancom. [Workbench, from Ieum 0018] A document
+    /// opened with its password keeps its memos read-only too: the app cannot write it back
+    /// with the password, so a memo change could never be kept.
     fn document_protected(&self) -> bool {
-        self.document.header.distribution
+        self.document.header.distribution || self.document.header.encrypted
     }
 
     fn memo_hits(&self) -> Vec<MemoHit> {
diff --git a/src/parser/hwpx/mod.rs b/src/parser/hwpx/mod.rs
index 7c7f57f03..2cef54b8c 100644
--- a/src/parser/hwpx/mod.rs
+++ b/src/parser/hwpx/mod.rs
@@ -952,7 +952,16 @@ pub(crate) fn parse_hwpx_with_reservation(
 /// 다시 쓰지 않고 종전 파서 결과를 그대로 반환한다.
 pub fn parse_hwpx_with_password(data: &[u8], password: &[u8]) -> Result<Document, HwpxError> {
     match crypto::decrypt_hwpx_package(data, password)? {
-        Some(decrypted) => parse_hwpx(&decrypted),
+        Some(decrypted) => {
+            let mut document = parse_hwpx(&decrypted)?;
+            // [Workbench, from Ieum 0018] The decrypted package is HWPX, but the fact that it
+            // needed a password must survive so editors keep it protected (the app refuses to
+            // write it without its password and keeps its memos read-only), as for a password
+            // HWP. The HWP5 writer clears the flag when it writes a plain document.
+            document.header.encrypted = true;
+            document.header.flags |= 0x02;
+            Ok(document)
+        }
         None => parse_hwpx(data),
     }
 }
diff --git a/tests/cases/issue_8017_memo_api.rs b/tests/cases/issue_8017_memo_api.rs
index a28624add..b28a3f6f1 100644
--- a/tests/cases/issue_8017_memo_api.rs
+++ b/tests/cases/issue_8017_memo_api.rs
@@ -567,31 +567,27 @@ fn memo_text_control_characters_are_normalized() {
     );
 }
 
-/// A password guards opening only: memos in an opened password document are
-/// editable, for HWP5 and HWPX alike, and opening does not touch the header.
+/// [Workbench, from Ieum 0018] A document opened with its password stays protected: HWP5 and
+/// HWPX alike say `encrypted`, and their memos are read-only (upstream: editable — a password
+/// only guards opening). The app cannot write a password document back with its password, so a
+/// memo change could never be kept; 0.8.7 with Ieum 0018 behaved so.
 #[test]
-fn memos_in_an_opened_password_document_are_editable() {
+fn memos_in_an_opened_password_document_are_read_only() {
     for rel in [
         "HWP5-password-123456.hwpx",
         "hwp3-sample16-hwp5-2024-password-123456.hwp",
     ] {
         let mut core = DocumentCore::from_bytes_with_password(&sample(rel), b"123456")
             .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!("open {rel}: {e}"));
-        let encrypted = core.document().header.encrypted;
+        assert!(core.document().header.encrypted, "{rel}");
         let first_text = core.document().sections[0]
             .paragraphs
             .iter()
             .position(|para| para.text.chars().count() >= 2)
             .expect("a paragraph with text");
-        let id = inserted_id(
-            &core
-                .insert_memo_at(0, first_text, 0, 2, "t", "body")
-                .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!("{rel}: {}", e.to_json())),
-        );
-        assert_eq!(memo_list(&core)[0]["editable"], true, "{rel}");
-        core.set_memo_text(id, "new").unwrap();
-        core.remove_memo(id).unwrap();
-        assert_eq!(core.document().header.encrypted, encrypted, "{rel}");
+        let error = core.insert_memo_at(0, first_text, 0, 2, "t", "body").unwrap_err();
+        assert_eq!(error.code.as_str(), "memo_read_only", "{rel}");
+        assert!(core.document().header.encrypted, "{rel}");
     }
 }
 
